The try/catch mechanism as seen in the previous example certainly takes error handling
to a higher technical level. But, from a practical perspective there is little that distinguishes
our tryCatchEx1 program from what was accomplished previously with exit or assert.
This is mostly a function of the simplicity of the example and, perhaps, makes the point
that when simple errors are expected a simple error handling method will su ffi ce. In more
complicated situations, the try/catch approach has many features that may make it worth
considering. For example, a function can throw objects as exception. This provides a con-
venient way to package diagnostic information that can be processed later in the catch
block. In this latter respect, there are many built-in exception classes that are used by
C++ functions and these can also be adapted to deal with some of the standard errors that
are produced by user-defined functions. In general, an exception class can be expected to
have a member function what that reports the form of the error. When creating new objects
from one of these classes, the argument supplied to the class constructor will determine the
output from what.
